window.MONSTER_CHUNK_8 = [{"name":"Skvader","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 size","hp":6,"hd":"1d10+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":3,"ref":4,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"poor"}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d3-1 plus attach)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","strength":8,"dexterity":15,"constitution":13,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":13,"charisma":6,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmd":10,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Stealth","value":14}],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate hills"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","family (3-12)","pack (1 wolpertinger and 3-12 skvaders)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This small creature has the forequarters and hind legs of a hare, and the wings and tail of a wood grouse.","source":"AP 61","special_abilities":[{"type":"Attach","kind":"Ex","details":"When a skvader hits with its bite attack, it automatically grapples its foe, dealing automatic bite damage each round. An attached skvader loses its Dexterity bonus to AC and has an AC of 12."}],"description":"A combination of hare and grouse, skyvaders boast features and mannerisms of both animals. At first glance, a skvader appears to be a common hare, though its shorter ears distinguish it from the longer-eared rabbits. Its wings, hindquarters, and tail usually have a coloration similar to its fur, and its birdlike features are only easily distinguishable up close. Skvaders are twitchy and nervous, constantly on the lookout for predators. They switch between standing stock still upon sensing danger and quickly bolting to escape. While generally docile, skvaders become dangerous if cornered. A skvader is about 1-1/2 ft. long and weighs around 10 pounds.\n\nEcology\n\nSkvaders live primarily in temperate forests, though they are also found in hillier regions with enough vegetation to provide them easy sustenance. They're also drawn to open, low-lying areas, particularly farm fields and plains covered in an abundance of tall grasses upon which they can feed. Their high metabolisms give these herbivores voracious appetites, which force them to consume such large quantities of plants that they pose significant threats to farmers' livelihoods. A family of skvaders infesting a farmer's fields can lay waste to his crops in just a couple of weeks. Once they've destroyed the vegetation in an area, the skyvaders move on to nearby farms and continue feeding. If cornered, a skvader emits a shrill screech that can be heard as far as a mile away. Other skvaders in the area immediately rush to its aid, causing a confrontation with a lone skvader to quickly escalate into a fight with a ferocious group of the creatures. When in combat, a skvader prefers to single out a threat and attach itself to that target. Scholars postulate that skvaders are a lesser form of their wolpertinger cousins, a variant that never fully developed into the greater threat the wolpertinger is. Others believe that the skvader is a distant precursor to the owlbear, citing the combination of mammalian and avian features, though on a much smaller scale. These scholars believe skvaders might have been an early experiment to combine two creatures into one, undertaken by the same lunatic wizard who supposedly created the owlbear.\n\nHabitat & Society\n\nTo protect themselves and secure a safe birthing environment, skyvaders often dig small burrows. A large enough skvader population frightens off other small herbivores, which leave their burrows behind for the skvaders to claim. They sometimes have to spend some time expanding the burrow's size, particularly if one or more wolpertingers are among the pack. In skvader families, adult females care for the young and the males dig the burrows, patrol for predators, and offer protection when their mates deliver a litter. Skvaders mate for life, though if one of a mated pair dies, the other seeks a new mate after a mourning period. Skvader young often stay with their parents for the first year of life. Multiple skvader families in the same region generally coexist peacefully. If one family grows significantly larger than the others, the smaller family simply moves on to new feeding grounds.\n\nSkvaders and Wolpertingers as Familiars\n\nWhile most skvaders and wolpertingers prefer the company of their families and packs, occasionally the more adventurous among them come to serve as familiars. Service to a powerful spellcaster brings with it protection and long-term companionship, which these creatures take advantage of. A neutral spellcaster can gain a skvader as a familiar at 3rd level by taking the Improved Familiar feat, and a neutral spellcaster can gain a wolpertinger as a familiar at 5th level by taking the Improved Familiar feat.","ecology":"Skvaders live primarily in temperate forests, though they are also found in hillier regions with enough vegetation to provide them easy sustenance. They're also drawn to open, low-lying areas, particularly farm fields and plains covered in an abundance of tall grasses upon which they can feed. Their high metabolisms give these herbivores voracious appetites, which force them to consume such large quantities of plants that they pose significant threats to farmers' livelihoods. A family of skvaders infesting a farmer's fields can lay waste to his crops in just a couple of weeks. Once they've destroyed the vegetation in an area, the skyvaders move on to nearby farms and continue feeding. If cornered, a skvader emits a shrill screech that can be heard as far as a mile away. Other skvaders in the area immediately rush to its aid, causing a confrontation with a lone skvader to quickly escalate into a fight with a ferocious group of the creatures. When in combat, a skvader prefers to single out a threat and attach itself to that target. Scholars postulate that skvaders are a lesser form of their wolpertinger cousins, a variant that never fully developed into the greater threat the wolpertinger is. Others believe that the skvader is a distant precursor to the owlbear, citing the combination of mammalian and avian features, though on a much smaller scale. These scholars believe skvaders might have been an early experiment to combine two creatures into one, undertaken by the same lunatic wizard who supposedly created the owlbear.","habitat_society":"To protect themselves and secure a safe birthing environment, skyvaders often dig small burrows. A large enough skvader population frightens off other small herbivores, which leave their burrows behind for the skvaders to claim. They sometimes have to spend some time expanding the burrow's size, particularly if one or more wolpertingers are among the pack. In skvader families, adult females care for the young and the males dig the burrows, patrol for predators, and offer protection when their mates deliver a litter. Skvaders mate for life, though if one of a mated pair dies, the other seeks a new mate after a mourning period. Skvader young often stay with their parents for the first year of life. Multiple skvader families in the same region generally coexist peacefully. If one family grows significantly larger than the others, the smaller family simply moves on to new feeding grounds.\n\nSkvaders and Wolpertingers as Familiars\n\nWhile most skvaders and wolpertingers prefer the company of their families and packs, occasionally the more adventurous among them come to serve as familiars. Service to a powerful spellcaster brings with it protection and long-term companionship, which these creatures take advantage of. A neutral spellcaster can gain a skvader as a familiar at 3rd level by taking the Improved Familiar feat, and a neutral spellcaster can gain a wolpertinger as a familiar at 5th level by taking the Improved Familiar feat."},{"name":"Sootwing Bat","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"CE","size":"Tiny","type":"undead","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"20 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural, +2 size","hp":7,"hd":"2d8-2","hdvalue":2,"fort":-1,"ref":1,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["channel resistance +2"],"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"good"}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d3-3 plus disease and paralysis)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"disease","details":"DC 10"},{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d4+1 rounds, DC 10, elves are immune to this effect"}],"strength":5,"dexterity":12,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":1,"cmb":0,"cmd":7,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":5,"special":"+9 when using blindsense"}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary","colony (4-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This batlike creature has jagged points at the tips of its wings and a long, lashing tongue.","source":"Monster Codex","special_abilities":[{"type":"Disease","kind":"Su","details":"Ghoul Fever"}],"description":"Ghoul spellcasters use these smaller cousins to skavelings as familiars. An evil spellcaster can gain a sootwing bat as a familiar at 5th level by taking the Improved Familiar feat. A sootwing bat familiar grants its master a +1 bonus to channel resistance (if the master has any).","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Stingray","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"30 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural","hp":13,"hd":"2d8+4","hdvalue":2,"fort":5,"ref":4,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":40}],"melee":"sting +1 (1d4 plus poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":10,"dexterity":13,"constitution":15,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":13,"charisma":2,"baseatk":1,"cmb":1,"cmd":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5,"special":"+13 in sand or mud"},{"skill":"Swim","value":8}],"environments":["warm oceans"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","flight (3-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A long tail fitted with a barbed stinger trails from this flat, triangular fish's body.","group":"Ray","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Sting-injury; save Fort DC 13; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect 1d2 Dex and 1 Con; cure 1 save. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Often found buried in mud, sand, or sea grass, this normally passive creature raises its tail above its body like a scorpion when cornered or injured. Its stinger is a barbed spine with two grooves allowing the toxin to enter the wound. While often found in bays and estuaries, some breeds of stingray can make their home in rivers far upstream from salt water. Stingrays, like their larger manta ray cousins, use a form of electrolocation to find prey in the silt-laden waters.\n\nThe stingray presented here is a relatively large member of the species; for smaller stingrays, apply the young creature template.\n\nStingray Companion Starting Statistics: Size Small; Speed swim 40 ft.; Attack sting (1d3 plus poison); Ability Scores Str 6, Dex 15, Con 13, Int 1, Wis 13, Cha 2; Special Qualities low-light vision.\n\n4th-Level Advancement: Size Medium; AC +1 natural armor; Attack sting (1d4 plus poison); Ability Scores Str +4, Dex -2, Con +2; Special Qualities blindsense 30 ft.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Stirge","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"magical beast","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +2 size","hp":5,"hd":"1d10","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":6,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":40,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"touch +7 (attach)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain"}],"strength":3,"dexterity":19,"constitution":10,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":1,"cmb":3,"cmb_special":"+8 vs. grapple when attached","cmd":9,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":16}],"sq":[{"type":"diseased"}],"environments":["temperate swamps","warm swamps"],"organizations":["solitary","colony (2-4)","flock (5-8)","storm (9-14)","swarm (15-40)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This insectoid creature has two pairs of bat wings, a tangle of thin legs, and a needle-sharp proboscis.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Attach","kind":"Ex","details":"When a stirge hits with a touch attack, its barbed legs latch onto the target, anchoring it in place. An attached stirge is effectively grappling its prey. The stirge loses its Dexterity bonus to AC and has an AC of 12, but holds on with great tenacity and inserts its proboscis into the grappled target's flesh. A stirge has a +8 racial bonus to maintain its grapple on a foe once it is attached. An attached stirge can be struck with a weapon or grappled itself-if its prey manages to win a grapple check or Escape Artist check against it, the stirge is removed."},{"type":"Blood Drain","kind":"Ex","details":"A stirge drains blood at the end of its turn if it is attached to a foe, inflicting 1 point of Constitution damage. Once a stirge has dealt 4 points of Constitution damage, it detaches and flies off to digest the meal. If its victim dies before the stirge's appetite has been sated, the stirge detaches and seeks a new target."},{"type":"Diseased","kind":"Ex","details":"Due to the stagnant swamps in which they live and their contact with the blood of numerous creatures, stirges are harbingers of disease. Any creature subjected to a stirge's blood drain attack has a 10% chance of being exposed to filth fever, blinding sickness, or a similar disease. Once this check is made, the victim can no longer be infected by this particular stirge, though attacks by different stirges are resolved normally and may result in multiple illnesses."}],"description":"Stirges are vicious, blood-drinking swamp pests that prey on wild animals, livestock, and unwary travelers. While weak individually, swarms of the creatures are capable of draining a man dry in minutes, leaving only a desiccated husk in their wake.\n\nCloser to mammals than insects, stirges carry their bodies through the air on four fleshy wings, searching out warm-blooded prey. They are fond of hiding near watering holes and waiting for travelers to drop their guard, then swooping out to attach and drink their fill by thrusting their long feeding tubes into unprotected veins. After feeding they flap heavily off into the mud and reeds to lay their eggs and rest until hunger drives them to hunt again.\n\nStirges are usually 1 foot long, with a wingspan of twice that, and weigh just under a pound. Their coloration is rust-red or reddish-brown with a dirty yellow underbelly, though stirges that have not fed in some time are often pale pink, their color deepening as they gorge.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Suli","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Suli","classes":[{"class":"ranger","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["native"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, +1 Dex","hp":11,"hd":"1d10+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":3,"will":-1,"resists":[{"type":"acid","value":5},{"type":"cold","value":5},{"type":"electricity","value":5},{"type":"fire","value":5}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"}],"melee":"scimitar +4 (1d6+3/18-20)","ranged":"shortbow +2 (1d6/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"elemental assault"},{"type":"favored enemy","details":"humans +2"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":12,"constitution":11,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":9,"charisma":10,"baseatk":1,"cmb":3,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Focus (scimitar)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":3},{"skill":"Knowledge (geography)","value":4},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":4},{"skill":"Ride","value":0},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":2},{"skill":"Survival","value":3}],"languages":["Common plus one elemental language"],"sq":[{"type":"track","details":"+1"},{"type":"wild empathy","details":"+1"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["single","pair","group (3-6)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This fine-featured humanoid wears exotic clothing and has flashing eyes; his arms are sheathed in a blaze of crackling flames.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Elemental Assault","kind":"Su","details":"Once per day as a swift action, a suli can call on the elemental power lurking in its veins to shroud its arms in acid, cold, electricity, or fire. Unarmed strikes with its elbows or hands (or attacks with weapons held in those hands) deal +1d6 points of damage of the appropriate energy type. This lasts for 1 round per level. The suli can end the effects of its elemental power early as a free action."}],"description":"Sulis (or suli-jann) are minor offshoots of the jann that live among humans. They are tall and look like ordinary men and women but for their abnormal beauty and the occasional flash of elemental light in their eyes. Though physically superior to and more attractive than normal humans, some suli-jann remain unaware of their genie heritage until later in life, when contact with a full-blooded genie draws forth a portion of their elemental power.\n\nWhile all sulis can trace their lineage back to a janni ancestor, very few have an immediate janni parent. In most cases, this legacy lies fallow in the blood for generations, only to emerge decades or even centuries later. Those whose powers become apparent early on usually lead troubled lives as youths, as the question of a child's origins tears some families apart; few of these unfortunate sulis grow up knowing anything of the peace of a happy home.\n\nSulis age at the same rate as humans and are physically identical to humans in terms of height and weight. During their youth, sulis tend to be slightly smaller than their fully human peers, but grow quickly to their full adult height upon reaching their teenage years. Sulis are impulsive and passionate by nature, and even those sulis who know nothing of their genie blood possess a measure of personal pride.","as_character":[{"description":"Sulis are defined by class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. Sulis have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Strength, +2 Charisma, -2 Intelligence: Sulis tend to be strong and charming, but slow-witted."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"Sulis can see twice as far as humans in dim light."},{"type":"Negotiator","value":"Sulis are keen negotiators, and gain a +2 racial bonus on Diplomacy and Sense Motive checks."},{"type":"Elemental Assault","value":"See above."},{"type":"Elemental Resistance","value":"Sulis have resistance to acid 5, cold 5, electricity 5, and fire 5."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Sulis begin play speaking Common and any one elemental language of their choice (Aquan, Auran, Ignan, or Terran). Sulis with high Intelligence scores can choose from among the following bonus languages: Aquan, Auran, Draconic, Ignan, and Terran."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"chainmail, scimitar, short bow with 20 arrows"},{"name":"Sylph","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Sylph","classes":[{"class":"rogue","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["native"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 armor, +3 Dex, +1 dodge","hp":9,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":0,"ref":5,"will":2,"resists":[{"type":"electricity","value":5}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"dagger -1 (1d4-1/19-20)","ranged":"dagger +3 (1d4-1/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"sneak attack","details":"+1d6"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":1,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":1,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"feather fall"}]}],"strength":8,"dexterity":17,"constitution":10,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":14,"charisma":10,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-1,"cmd":13,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":7},{"skill":"Appraise","value":6},{"skill":"Bluff","value":4},{"skill":"Climb","value":3},{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":4},{"skill":"Knowledge (local)","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Sleight of Hand","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":7},{"skill":"Use Magic Device","value":4}],"languages":["Auran","Common","Elven","Halfling"],"sq":[{"type":"air affinity"},{"type":"trapfinding","details":"+1"}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (3-6)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This pale, waifish woman, her hair waving in a nonexistent breeze, looks as if she might fade away into mist entirely at any minute.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Air Affinity","kind":"Ex","details":"Sylph sorcerers with the elemental (air) bloodline treat their Charisma score as 2 points higher for all sorcerer spells and class abilities. Sylph clerics with the Air domain cast their domain powers and spells at +1 caster level."}],"description":"Sylphs are humans whose family trees include elemental beings of air, such as djinn. They tend to be pale and thin to the point of appearing delicate, though their skinny bodies are more resilient than they look. While many can pass unnoticed through crowds of humans, sylphs display their heritage in subtle ways, and those who study them carefully sometimes notice that breezes seem to follow a sylph wherever she goes, even inside rooms with no windows. When consumed by fits of anger or passion, these tendencies become more apparent, as winds surround the sylph and tousle her hair or knock small items from shelves. Many sylphs have complex markings on their pale flesh that resemble tiny swirling designs like blue and gray tattoos, and the most exotic of their kind have hair that twists and coils almost as if it were made of living mist.\n\nAs people, sylphs tend to be shy and reclusive, blending into crowds or skillfully avoiding those they don't desire to meet. Yet while they often prefer to manipulate situations and avoid conf lict themselves, most sylphs remain intensely curious about other people, and often go to great lengths to spy or eavesdrop on those who spark their interest (a hobby frequently referred to as \"listening to the wind\"). This combined love of subterfuge and ability to slip away from any compromising situation makes sylphs perfectly suited to lives as rogues, thieves, and spies, and beneath the average sylph's veneer of shy waifishness lies a mind that's capable and calculating, constantly sizing up the competition and analyzing the most effective exits from any given room.","as_character":[{"description":"Sylphs are defined by class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. Sylphs have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Intelligence, -2 Constitution: Sylphs are quick and insightful, but slight and delicate."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Sylphs can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Sylph Magic","value":"Feather fall 1/day (caster level equals the sylph's Hit Dice)."},{"type":"Energy Resistance","value":"Sylphs have electricity resistance 5."},{"type":"Air Affinity","value":"See above."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Sylphs begin play speaking Common and Auran. Sylphs with high Intelligence scores can choose any of the following bonus languages: Aquan, Dwarven, Elven, Gnome, Halfling, Ignan, and Terran."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"leather armor, two daggers"},{"name":"Tengu","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"tengu","classes":[{"class":"rogue","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["tengu"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 armor, +3 Dex","hp":9,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":1,"ref":5,"will":14,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"dagger +3 (1d4+1/19-20), bite -2 (1d3)","ranged":"short bow +3 (1d6/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"sneak attack","details":"+1d6"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":17,"constitution":12,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":15,"charisma":8,"baseatk":0,"cmb":20,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":30,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":7},{"skill":"Appraise","value":4},{"skill":"Bluff","value":3},{"skill":"Climb","value":5},{"skill":"Knowledge (local)","value":4},{"skill":"Linguistics","value":8},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Goblin","Halfling","Tengu"],"sq":[{"type":"gifted linguist"},{"type":"swordtrained"},{"type":"trapfinding"}],"environments":["temperate mountains","temperate urban"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","conspiracy (3-12)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Black feathers cover this crow-headed humanoid, and its hands and legs end in powerful talons.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Gifted Linguist","kind":"Ex","details":"Tengus gain a +4 racial bonus on Linguistics checks, and learn 2 languages each time they gain a rank in Linguistics rather than 1 language."},{"type":"Swordtrained","kind":"Ex","details":"Tengus are trained from birth in swordplay, and as a result are automatically proficient with sword-like weapons (including bastard swords, daggers, elven curve blades, falchions, greatswords, kukris, longswords, punching daggers, rapiers, scimitars, short swords, and two-bladed swords)."}],"description":"Tengus are a race of avian humanoids that resemble crows or ravens, and often bear much of the same stigma. Though they frequently choose to live among other races in densely populated cities, their society is tight and closed, and they rarely allow others to see its inner workings. Tengus often band together in small groups to create roosts in vacant warehouses or condemned buildings, and these raucous gathering places are generally assumed to be thieves' guilds by outsiders-an assumption that's correct roughly half the time. Like the crows they share physical traits with, tengus are naturally covetous, especially of shiny or colorful items, and prone to bouts of good-natured kleptomania if they don't carefully keep themselves in check. Vain and prideful creatures, they are easily persuaded with flattery.","as_character":[{"description":"Tengus are defined by their class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. All tengus have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, -2 Constitution, +2 Wisdom: Tengus are fast and observant, but relatively fragile and delicate."},{"type":"Senses","value":"Tengus have low-light vision."},{"type":"Sneaky","value":"Tengus gain a +2 racial bonus on Perception and Stealth."},{"type":"Gifted Linguist","value":"See above."},{"type":"Sword trained","value":"See above."},{"type":"Natural Weapon","value":"Tengus possess a bite natural attack that inflicts 1d3 points of damage on a hit. This is a primary attack, or a secondary attack if the tengu wields a manufactured weapon."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Tengus begin play speaking Common and their own dialect of Tengu. Tengus with high Intelligence scores can choose any language as a bonus language."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"leather armor, dagger, short bow and 20 arrows"},{"name":"Thylacine","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"animal","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 size","hp":7,"hd":"1d8+3","hdvalue":1,"fort":5,"ref":4,"will":7,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +2 (1d4+1/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":12,"dexterity":15,"constitution":16,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":7,"baseatk":0,"cmb":4,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":8}],"sq":[{"type":"powerful jaws"}],"environments":["warm hills"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This catlike creature has a striped pelt and whiskers that jut from a long muzzle filled with sharp teeth.","group":"Marsupial","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Powerful Jaws","kind":"Ex","details":"A thylacine's muscular jaws threaten a critical hit on a natural roll of 19 or 20."}],"description":"These carnivorous, wide-jawed marsupials typically hunt at night and have a reputation for savagery. By day, they nest in hollow trees or clusters of bushes.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Tiefling","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Tiefling","classes":[{"class":"rogue","level":1}],"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["native"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+3 armor, +3 Dex","hp":10,"hd":"1d8+2","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":5,"will":3,"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":5},{"type":"electricity","value":5},{"type":"fire","value":5}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"short sword +3 (1d6+1/19-20)","ranged":"light crossbow +3 (1d8/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"sneak attack","details":"+1d6"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":1,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":3,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"darkness"}]}],"strength":13,"dexterity":17,"constitution":14,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":0,"cmb":11,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":23,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":6},{"skill":"Bluff","value":4},{"skill":"Disable Device","value":6},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":6},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":2},{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":5},{"skill":"Sleight of Hand","value":6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8}],"languages":["Abyssal","Common","Infernal"],"sq":[{"type":"fiendish sorcery"},{"type":"trapfinding"}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (3-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This lanky man sneers as he draws his sword. Tiny horns and a barbed tail reveal that he is something more than human.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fiendish Sorcery","kind":"Ex","details":"Tiefling sorcerers with the Abyssal or Infernal bloodline treat their Charisma score as 2 points higher for all sorcerer class abilities."}],"description":"Tieflings are humans with demonic, devilish, or other evil outsider blood in their ancestry. Often persecuted for their strange appearance and unnatural mannerisms, most Tieflings disguise their nature or are forced to live on the fringes or underbelly of civilized society. Though they are not born evil, it is an easy path for them to find, especially as most suffer at the hands of \"normal\" folks while growing up. Tieflings look mostly human except for some physical traits that reveal their strange heritage.","as_character":[{"description":"Tieflings are defined by their class levels-they don't possess racial HD. They have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Intelligence, -2 Charisma: Tieflings are quick in body and mind, but are inherently strange."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Tieflings see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Skilled","value":"Tieflings have a +2 racial bonus on Bluff and Stealth checks."},{"type":"Spell-Like Ability","value":"Tieflings can use darkness once per day as a spell-like ability. The caster level for this ability equals the tief ling's class level."},{"type":"Fiendish Resistance","value":"Tieflings have cold resistance 5, electricity resistance 5, and fire resistance 5."},{"type":"Fiendish Sorcery","value":"See above."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Tieflings begin play speaking Common and either Abyssal or Infernal. Tieflings with high Intelligence scores can choose any of the following: Abyssal, Draconic, Dwarven, Elven, Gnome, Goblin, Halfling, Infernal, and Orc."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"studded leather armor, short sword, light crossbow with 20 bolts"},{"name":"Triaxian","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Triaxian","classes":[{"class":"ranger","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["triaxian"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, +2 Dex","hp":13,"hd":"1d10+3","hdvalue":1,"fort":4,"ref":4,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"lance +2 (1d8+1/x3) or  longsword +2 (1d8+1/19-20)","ranged":"composite longbow +3 (1d8+1/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"favored enemy","details":"dragons +2"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":14,"constitution":15,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":14,"charisma":8,"baseatk":1,"cmb":20,"cmd":29,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Mounted Archery"},{"feat":"Mounted Combat"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Knowledge (arcana)","value":1},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":4},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Ride","value":1},{"skill":"Stealth","value":1},{"skill":"Survival","value":6}],"languages":["Triaxian"],"sq":[{"type":"seasoned"},{"type":"track","details":"+1"},{"type":"wild empathy","details":"+0"}],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This warrior looks like an attractive humanoid with pointed, featherlike ears and eyes shaped into long, horizontal slits. Though he has no true hair, his entire body is covered in short, sleek white fur.","source":"AP 70","special_abilities":[{"type":"Seasoned","kind":"Ex","details":"Triaxians suffer no harm from being in hot or cold environments depending on whether they are Summerborn or Winterborn. Summerborn Triaxians can exist comfortably in conditions between 90 and 140 degrees Fahrenheit without having to attempt Fortitude saves. Winterborn Triaxians can exist comfortably in conditions between 40 and -20 degrees Fahrenheit without having to attempt Fortitude saves. When in conditions of severe cold or heat, Triaxians only have to attempt Fortitude saves once per hour instead of once every 10 minutes. Transitional Triaxians don't have this ability."}],"description":"Triaxians are the dominant race on the planet Triaxus, a world whose erratic orbit causes exceptionally long and disparate seasons. Though eerily similar to humans, elves, and the other mammalian humanoids common on Golarion, Triaxians have developed certain adaptations to their environment that mark them as indisputably alien.\n\nJust like human ethnicities, Triaxian populations vary in size, weight, and other distinguishing physical characteristics, based primarily on the geographical regions in which they reside. Most Triaxians, both males and females, are around 6 ft. tall but somewhat lean compared to humans, rarely weighing more than 200 pounds. Their ears are elongated, but rather than being pointed like elves or half lings, these appendages are instead notched in a feathered or comblike pattern. The flaps created by these notches constantly move to adjust the ear's shape, operating both consciously and unconsciously to help Triaxians focus on specific sounds, not unlike the maneuverable ears of dogs, cats, and other such animals.\n\nLike many creatures on their world, Triaxians have evolved to shift along with their environment, resulting in differences between generations of the same family that would seem bizarre to humanoids from Golarion. In the warm summer years, Triaxians are completely hairless, with skin that ranges from deep red to coffee-colored to charcoal black. This configuration allows them to better survive in the sweltering heat of the planet's tropical summers, with the increased melanin in their dark skin protecting them from the sun's intense rays. These Summerborn Triaxians, as they are known, breed true for many generations-yet as the planet begins its rapid seasonal shift, so do the Triaxians. Newborn Triaxians begin to evince new adaptations to the cooling environment, and by the time winter has come on in full, Triaxians change markedly: their pale bodies are covered in fine, insulating white fur like that of an ermine, while their eyes narrow to elongated slits to protect against snowblindness. These new traits similarly breed true until the seasons begin to change once more, at which point the eyes widen and fur recedes, starting the cycle anew.\n\nJust as Triaxians differ physically depending on which season they're born into, so do their cultures and customs change. Winterborn Triaxians are defined by the hardship of a world whose very environment seeks to starve or freeze them. They tend to be stolid, hardworking people, with an ironclad sense of honor stemming from the knowledge that in a Triaxian winter, a broken promise can mean death for a whole clan. Even within large communities, the focus remains on survival for one's family and friends, with individuals willing and ready to share, serve, and die to protect the group. Oaths of friendship are serious affairs; once given they are rarely transgressed, and doing so risks a blood feud. With the exception of those nomadic hunter tribes that migrate in pursuit of herd animals, most settlements are permanent and fortified against the predatory horrors that stalk the blinding blizzards.\n\nSummerborn Triaxians, by contrast, are born into a world of plenty. With forests full of fruit and game, and glaciers receding to reveal vast tracts of fertile earth, the attachment to cold stone fortresses and cramped cities weakens, and Triaxians spread out across the landscape. For many, it is a time of nomadism, living in small bands and temporary structures, or else participating in great waves of land-grabs as homesteaders. Though they still have the weight of history to remind them of the hardships of their ancestors-and those yet to come for their descendants- Summerborn Triaxians tend to be an easy-going, generous people, quick to accept outsiders, to break with their clans and families and strike out on their own, and to challenge convention in pursuit of greater goals. They are simultaneously less hardened and more likely to go to war, for with the business of survival taking less energy and new territory constantly being settled, leaders have more time and resources to dispute borders and jockey for power. Historically, Summerborn Triaxians are those most likely to visit other planes or worlds, as the drive to explore their newly thawed planet also leads them to look beyond it.\n\nTransitional Triaxians occupy a much smaller portion of the Triaxian adaptation cycle than either the Summerborn or Winterborn-usually no more than a generation each orbital year-and often play an uncomfortable role in their society. To Summerborn Triaxians, a Transitional child is an ill omen-a sign that winter approaches, and that the time of plenty is drawing to a close. The first such individuals to appear are often hidden or slain by their parents, and vilified by those rebellious fools and leaders who believe that Transitional children actively bring on the winter, or who simply don't want to begin the long and arduous process of preparing for future generations' survival. Transitional children born at the tail of winter, on the other hand, are often treasured and held up as hopeful signs of a golden era to come. Yet even these may face persecution by those dynasties that fear change, or whose leaders expect a loss of power once their people are free to spread beyond their reach. In both cases, Transitional Triaxians find themselves in a society where they are visibly different from both their parents and their children, and where their very existence is a weighty portent.\n\nStrangely, while Triaxians' mutable nature is perfectly adapted to their planet's erratic orbit, the cycle of physiological changes seems keyed to internal clocks rather than external indicators. Even in situations where these adaptations would be disadvantageous, the small populations of Triaxians who have set up residence on other worlds or planes continue to change in time with their kindred on Triaxus.\n\nBeyond their obvious adaptations to their environment, Triaxians are remarkably human. Their basic biology, social structures, cultures, and philosophy-while sometimes surprising to outsiders-all fall well within the bounds of what might be encountered in a humanoid race on Golarion, thus making them one of the least \"alien\" civilizations in Golarion's solar system. Though the statistics above represent a typical initiate training to become a Dragonrider of the Skyfire Mandate, this is by no means the standard for the race-outside of broad strokes, it's as impossible to describe Triaxians' myriad traits and predispositions as it would be to do so for humanity itself. Triaxians are good and evil, warlike and peaceful, magical and mundane, and everything in between.","as_character":[{"description":"Triaxians don't possess racial Hit Dice, gaining their capabilities instead from class levels. All Triaxians have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Constitution, +2 Wisdom, -2 Strength: Triaxians are a hardy and wily race, as befits their constantly changing environment, but their lean forms have trouble maintaining large amounts of muscle mass."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"In dim light, Triaxians can see twice as far as humans."},{"type":"Keen Senses","value":"Triaxians' unique ear construction grants them a +2 bonus on Perception checks."},{"type":"Bonus Feat","value":"Triaxians select one extra feat at 1st level."},{"type":"Seasoned","value":"See above."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Triaxians speak Triaxian (the common trade language of Triaxus). Triaxians with high Intelligence scores can choose any languages they want (except secret languages, such as Druidic)."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"chainmail, composite longbow with 20 arrows, lance, longsword"},{"name":"Ulfen Raider","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Human","classes":[{"class":"barbarian","level":1}],"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["human"],"init":1,"ac":17,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, +1 Dex, -2 rage, +2 shield","hp":17,"hd":"1d12+5","hdvalue":1,"fort":6,"ref":1,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"battleaxe +6 (1d8+4/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rage","details":"6 rounds/day"}],"strength":19,"dexterity":12,"constitution":18,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":1,"cmb":5,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (battleaxe)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":3},{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Profession (sailor)","value":2},{"skill":"Survival","value":5},{"skill":"Swim","value":1}],"languages":["Common","Skald"],"sq":[{"type":"fast movement"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","raiding party (2d6 Ulfen raiders and one longboat captain)"],"treasure":"NPC Gear (chainmail, heavy wooden shield, battleaxe, potion of cure light wounds)","description_visual":"This fur-clad and fiercely rugged barbarian stands ready to do battle with a deadly looking battleaxe clenched in one hand.","source":"Lands Of The Linnorm Kings","description":"Raiders from the Linnorm Kingdoms seek to prove themselves by sailing to distant lands and returning with riches, either obtained by shrewd trading or taken as plunder from a successful raid. All raiders seek to show their courage in battle, fighting fiercely for pillage or honor.\n\nWhile raiding, these barbarians employ the element of surprise; in the faint predawn light, they quietly row their longship with muff led oars as they draw near the targeted settlement. Sometimes Ulfen raiders might put in at a nearby, uninhabited harbor so as to approach their quarry on foot from inland, using nearby terrain as cover. However they approach, the raiders aim to catch their prize unawares to prevent their prey from fleeing with valuable plunder. When a group of raiders attacks, the clamorous sound of their Ulfen war songs floats over the din of battle, and the battle plan changes from stealth to intimidation. While Ulfen raiders have a keen sense of tactics, they prefer to fight as bold individuals, rather than in strict formations, in hopes of gaining personal glory. Once battle commences, Ulfen raiders often form a wedge of spears to drive their way through enemy lines. They use shield wall tactics when outnumbered or when they encounter stiff opposition.\n\nAfter a successful attack, Ulfen raiders quickly gather loot and valuable captives onto their longships. As well as coins and other riches, the raiders often take bulky luxury goods that are hard to obtain in their homeland, such as well-made furniture or carpets, and even appropriate high-pedigree livestock to increase the quality of their own herds. Slaves are often harvested from the ranks of a village's able-bodied youths. While Ulfen raiders seize anything of value that they can bring on board their longships, they are unlikely to wantonly destroy a raided settlement. After all, if the settlement is able to eventually recover from the attack, it can provide more plunder in the next raiding season.\n\nIn large groups, Ulfen raiders can take over a wide area of countryside, and often demand an exorbitant payment from local leaders to move on. Smaller communities frequently pay, rather than attempting the expensive process of raising an army and then fighting an uncertain battle against these bloodthirsty raiders.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Undine","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Undine","classes":[{"class":"cleric","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["native"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+5 armor, +2 Dex","hp":8,"hd":"1d8","hdvalue":1,"fort":1,"ref":2,"will":5,"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":5}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"trident +0 (1d8)","ranged":"sling +2 (1d4)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"channel positive energy","details":"7/day, DC 12, 1d6"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":1,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":0,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"hydraulic push"}]},{"frequency":"6/day","spells":[{"spell":"dazing touch"},{"spell":"icicle","details":"1d6+1 cold damage"}]}],"spellsprepared_caster":"cleric","spellsprepared_caster_level":1,"spellsprepared_concentration":0,"spellsprepared":[{"spell_level":1,"spell_slots":1,"spells":[{"spell":"bless"},{"spell":"charm person","type":"domain"},{"spell":"divine favor"}]},{"spell_level":0,"spell_slots":99,"spells":[{"spell":"create water"},{"spell":"guidance"},{"spell":"stabilize"}]}],"spelldomains":"Charm, Water","strength":11,"dexterity":14,"constitution":8,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":17,"charisma":14,"baseatk":0,"cmb":0,"cmd":12,"feats":[{"feat":"Extra Channel"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":6},{"skill":"Knowledge (religion)","value":4},{"skill":"Swim","value":4}],"languages":["Aquan","Common"],"sq":[{"type":"water affinity"}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (3-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This blue-haired, blue-skinned man moves with a liquid grace. His ears are fin-like, and his hands and ft. are webbed.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 2","special_abilities":[{"type":"Water Affinity","kind":"Ex","details":"Undine sorcerers with the elemental (water) bloodline treat their Charisma score as 2 points higher for all sorcerer spells and class abilities. Undine clerics with the Water domain cast their Water domain powers and spells at +1 caster level."}],"description":"Undines are humans whose ancestry includes elemental beings of water, such as marids. This connection with the Plane of Water is most noticeably manifested in their coloration, which tends to mimic that of lakes or oceans-all undines have limpid, blue eyes, and their skin and hair can range from pale blue-white to the deep blue or green of the sea.","as_character":[{"description":"Undines are defined by class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. Undines have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Wisdom, -2 Strength: Undines are both perceptive and agile, but tend to adapt rather than match force with force."},{"type":"Speed","value":"Undines have a swim speed of 30 ft."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Undines can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Spell-Like Abilities","value":"Hydraulic push 1/day. (Caster level equals the undine's total Hit Dice.)"},{"type":"Energy Resistance","value":"Undines have cold resistance 5."},{"type":"Water Affinity","value":"See above."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Undines begin play speaking Common and Aquan. Undines with high Intelligence scores can choose any of the following bonus languages: Auran, Dwarven, Elven, Gnome, Halfling, Ignan, and Terran."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"scale mail, trident"},{"name":"Vanara","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Vanara","classes":[{"class":"monk","level":1}],"alignment":"LG","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["vanara"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":17,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 dodge, +3 Wis","hp":9,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":5,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"quarterstaff +1 (1d6+1) or flurry of blows +0 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"flurry of blows"},{"type":"stunning fist","details":"1/day, DC 13"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":16,"constitution":10,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":17,"charisma":6,"baseatk":0,"cmb":1,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Combat Reflexes","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Improved Unarmed Strike","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Stunning Fist","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":9},{"skill":"Climb","value":13},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan","Vanaran"],"sq":[{"type":"prehensile tail"}],"environments":["any forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","party","(3-6)","community (7-100)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This lean, simian humanoid is covered in a layer of soft, lightly colored fur and has a long, prehensile tail.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Prehensile Tail","kind":"Ex","details":"All vanaras have long, flexible tails that they can use to carry objects. They cannot wield weapons with their tails, but the tails do allow them to retrieve small stowed objects carried on their persons as a swift action."}],"description":"Vanaras are intelligent, simian humanoids who live in deep forests and jungles. They are both agile and clever, but saddled with boundless curiosity and a love of pranks that, while normally harmless, hinder ingratiations with those they encounter. A vanara's body is covered in a thin coat of soft fur, and individuals with chestnut, ivory, and even golden coats are common. Despite its fur, a vanara can grow lengthy hair on its head just like a human, and both male and female vanaras take pains to wear elaborate hairstyles for important social functions. The hair on a vanara's head matches the color of its fur. All vanaras have long, prehensile tails and handlike ft. capable of well-articulated movements. A vanara stands slightly shorter than a typical human. Males weigh from 150 to 200 pounds at most, with females weighing slightly less. Vanaras live for 60 to 75 years.\n\nVanaras live in large, tree-top villages connected by rope-bridges and ladders. Homes are carved out of trees but usually left open to the elements except for woven leaf canopies and overhangs. Vanara villages are typically led by the community's religious leader-usually a cleric, oracle, or monk.","as_character":[{"description":"Vanaras are defined by their class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. All vanaras have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Wisdom, -2 Charisma: Vanaras are agile and insightful, but are also rather mischievous."},{"type":"Climb Speed","value":"Vanaras have a climb speed of 20 ft."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"Vanaras can see twice as far as a human in conditions of dim light."},{"type":"Nimble","value":"Vanaras have a +2 racial bonus on Acrobatics and Stealth checks."},{"type":"Prehensile Tail","value":"See above."},{"type":"Languages","value":"A vanara begins play speaking both Common and Vanaran. Vanaras with high Int scores can choose from among the following bonus languages: Aklo, Celestial, Elven, Gnome, Goblin, and Sylvan."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"quarterstaff"},{"name":"Vegepygmy","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"plant","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural, +1 size","hp":5,"hd":"1d8+1","hdvalue":1,"fort":3,"ref":2,"will":0,"defensive_abilities":["plant traits"],"dr":"5/slashing or bludgeoning","immunities":["electricity"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +1 (1d4) or longspear +1 (1d6)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":11,"dexterity":14,"constitution":12,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-1,"cmd":11,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10,"special":"+18 in vegetation"}],"languages":["Undercommon","Vegepygmy (cannot speak)"],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-6)","tribe (7-30 plus 1 chieftain)"],"treasure":"standard (longspear)","description_visual":"Short, thin, and green, this small humanoid wields a spear. Tendrils of fungus dangle from its arms, midsection, and legs.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","description":"Vegepygmys are created by a dangerous fungus known as russet mold; creatures slain by russet mold serve as incubators for the mold's spores, and a day later, the dead body bursts to release 1d6 fully grown vegepygmys. A vegepygmy has no real relation to the body from which it emerges, but the creature may yet retain a strange sort of reverence for its \"birth corpse\" and carry with it a grisly memento from the body, typically a few bones or teeth. Often, a vegepygmy uses such bones to craft a crude spear or some other weapon. Vegepygmy tribes live in tightly knit communities and fiercely guard their patches of russet mold. Vegepygmies cannot speak-they communicate via a crude language of rhythmic taps, beats, and clicks. Hunting parties echo these exchanges through the caves they travel. Vegepygmies stand 2 to 4 ft. tall and weigh between 15 to 45 pounds. Approximately 1 in 20 vegepymies are chieftains. A vegepygmy chieftain is an advanced vegepygmy (often one with class levels) that carries an infestation of russet mold spores in its flesh. A vegepygmy chieftain infects any creature it hits with its natural weapons with russet mold spores. Russet Mold (CR 6) This hazardous fungus can be found in dark, wet areas, and often grows in great abundance at the heart of a vegepygmy lair. When a creature approaches within 5 ft. of a patch of russet mold, the fungus releases a cloud of spores in a 5-foot radius burst. Everyone in the area must make a DC 15 Fortitude save or the spores quickly take root in their victims, inf licting 2 points of Constitution damage per round. A new Fortitude save can be attempted each round to halt the growth. Although immunity to disease won't protect against russet mold spores, the growth can be halted by remove disease and similar effects. Exposure to sunlight also halts the spores' growth. Plants are immune to russet mold spores. After 24 hours, a fully grown vegepygmy bursts from the body of any creature slain by russet mold, provided the creature was Small or larger. For each size category larger than Small, the body produces one additional vegepygmy. A patch of russet mold is unharmed by all effects save for acid, alcohol, or remove disease (or a similar magical effect, such as heal), all of which can kill a single patch of russet mold on contact. Sunlight doesn't kill the mold, but does render it dormant and harmless as long as the sunlight persists.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Viper","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"animal","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 natural, +2 size","hp":3,"hd":"1d8-1","hdvalue":1,"fort":1,"ref":5,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":20},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d2-2 plus poison)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","strength":4,"dexterity":17,"constitution":8,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":13,"charisma":2,"baseatk":0,"cmb":1,"cmd":8,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":15},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Presented here are the base animal statistics for all of the most commonly used familiars-of course, these statistics can also be used for normal animals as well. Small animals like these use Dexterity to modify Climb and Swim checks.","group":"Familiar","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save Fort DC 9; frequency 1/round for 6 rounds; effect 1d2 Con; cure 1 save."}],"description":"Vipers are not particularly aggressive snakes, but their poisonous bite can be deadly.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Vishkanya","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Vishkanya","classes":[{"class":"ninja","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["vishkanya"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":10,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex","hp":11,"hd":"1d8+3","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":5,"will":0,"save_mods":"+1 vs. poison","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"kukri +1 (1d4+1/18-20 plus poison)","ranged":"shuriken +3 (1d2+1 plus poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"poison","details":"DC 12"},{"type":"sneak attack","details":"+1d6"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":17,"constitution":14,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":10,"charisma":12,"baseatk":0,"cmb":1,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Deceitful"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":7},{"skill":"Bluff","value":7},{"skill":"Disguise","value":7},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Vishkanya"],"sq":[{"type":"poison use"},{"type":"toxic"}],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","squad (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This lithe woman wears loose-fitting robes and wields a cruel-looking blade. Her eyes are pure white orbs.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison Use","kind":"Ex","details":"Vishkanyas are skilled with poison and never accidentally poison themselves when using or applying poison."},{"type":"Toxic","kind":"Ex","details":"A number of times per day equal to its Constitution modifier (minimum 1/day), a vishkanya can envenom a weapon that it wields with its toxic saliva or blood (using blood requires the vishkanya to be injured when it uses this ability). Applying venom in this way is a swift action. Vishkanya Venom: Injury; save Fort DC 10 + 1/2 the vishkanya's Hit Dice + the vishkanya's Constitution modifier; frequency 1/round for 6 rounds; effect 1d2 Dex; cure 1 save."}],"description":"Vishkanyas are a race of exotic humanoids known for their guile and affinity for poisons of all kinds. A vishkanya's flesh is made up of fine scales that from a distance of even a few ft. look just like particularly smooth skin. These scales are usually a single dark color, although some of them have complex patterns like stripes or even spirals. A vishkanya's tongue is forked like a serpent's tongue, and its eyes lack visible pupils.\n\nAlthough legends abound that the merest touch from a vishkanya can slay a mortal humanoid, these tales are patently false. A vishkanya's skin is no more poisonous than that of any human, but it is true that their blood, spit, and other bodily fluids can be dangerous. Vishkanyas are skilled in using their own spittle or even their blood to envenom their weapons, and those who fight them should be wary of exposure to the vishkanya's poison. A vishkanya is 6 ft. tall and weighs 130 pounds.","as_character":[{"description":"Vishkanyas are defined by their class levels; they don't possess racial Hit Dice. Vishkanyas have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Charisma, -2 Wisdom: Vishkanyas are graceful and elegant, but often irrational."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"Vishkanyas can see twice as far as humans in conditions of dim light."},{"type":"Keen Senses","value":"Vishkanyas receive a +2 racial bonus on Perception checks."},{"type":"Limber","value":"Vishkanyas gain a +2 racial bonus on Escape Artist and Stealth checks."},{"type":"Poison Resistance","value":"A vishkanya has a racial bonus on saves against poison equal to its Hit Dice."},{"type":"Poison Use","value":"See above."},{"type":"Toxic","value":"See above."},{"type":"Weapon Familiarity","value":"Vishkanyas are always proficient with blowguns, kukri, and shuriken."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Vishkanyas begin play speaking Common and Vishkanya. Vishkanyas with high Intelligence scores can choose any of the following bonus languages: Aklo, Draconic, Elven, Goblin, Sylvan, and Undercommon."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"kukri, 10 shuriken"},{"name":"Vulture","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"animal","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +1 natural, +1 size","hp":6,"hd":"1d8+2","hdvalue":1,"fort":6,"ref":3,"will":1,"save_mods":"+4 vs. disease","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"fly","speed":50,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"bite +2 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":12,"dexterity":13,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":7,"baseatk":0,"cmb":0,"cmd":11,"feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":7},{"skill":"Perception","value":9}],"environments":["warm hills","warm plains"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","flock (3-24)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This dark-feathered vulture has a bald neck and head, and its long, hooked beak is caked with carrion.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 3","description":"Though related to raptors and other birds of prey, vultures mainly feed on carrion they spot or scent from on high. Vultures have weak claws compared to predatory birds, but their sharp and wickedly hooked beaks tear living flesh as readily as the sharpest blade.\n\nDespite dining on carrion, vultures prefer their corpses newly dead rather than rotten. Some even hunt smaller prey when food is scarce.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Wayang","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"wayang","classes":[{"class":"illusionist","level":1}],"alignment":"CN","size":"Small","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["wayang"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 size","hp":8,"hd":"1d6+2","hdvalue":1,"fort":1,"ref":3,"will":1,"save_mods":"+2 vs. shadow spells","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"dagger +0 (1d3-1/19-20)","ranged":"blowgun +4 (1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":1,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":6,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"ghost sound"},{"spell":"pass without trace"},{"spell":"ventriloquism"}]},{"frequency":"6/day","spells":[{"spell":"blinding ray"}]}],"spellsprepared_caster":"illusionist","spellsprepared_caster_level":1,"spellsprepared_concentration":6,"spellsprepared":[{"spell_level":1,"spell_slots":1,"spells":[{"spell":"hypnotism"},{"spell":"silent image"},{"spell":"vanish"}]},{"spell_level":0,"spell_slots":99,"spells":[{"spell":"detect magic"},{"spell":"ghost sound"},{"spell":"read magic"}]}],"strength":8,"dexterity":16,"constitution":12,"intelligence":17,"wisdom":8,"charisma":13,"baseatk":0,"cmb":-2,"cmd":11,"feats":[{"feat":"Combat Casting"},{"feat":"Scribe Scroll"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Craft (alchemy)","value":7},{"skill":"Knowledge (arcana)","value":7},{"skill":"Perception","value":2},{"skill":"Spellcraft","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10}],"languages":["Abyssal","Common","Draconic","Goblin","Wayang"],"sq":[{"type":"arcane bond","details":"amulet"},{"type":"extended illusions","details":"+1 round"},{"type":"light and dark"},{"type":"shadow magic"}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","tribe (3-24)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Spiralling patterns cover this shadowy humanoid's skin, and its black hair trails away in wisps.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","special_abilities":[{"type":"Light and Dark","kind":"Su","details":"Once per day as an immediate action, a wayang can choose to be affected by positive and negative energy effects as if it were an undead creature, taking damage from positive energy and healing damage from negative energy. This ability lasts for 1 minute."}],"description":"Originating from the Shadow Plane, wayangs are pixie-like in stature with extremely gangly limbs and skin the color of deep shadow. They follow a philosophy known as \"The Dissolution,\" which teaches that in passing they again merge into shadow.","as_character":[{"description":"Wayangs are defined by class levels-they don't have racial Hit Dice. Wayangs have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Intelligence, -2 Wisdom: Wayangs are nimble and cagey, but their worldview is strange."},{"type":"Small","value":"Wayangs are Small creatures and gain a +1 size bonus to AC, a +1 size bonus on attack rolls, a -1 penalty on combat maneuver checks and to CMD, and a +4 size bonus on Stealth checks."},{"type":"Slow Speed","value":"Wayangs have a base speed of 20 ft."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Wayangs can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Light and Dark (Su)","value":"See stat block above."},{"type":"Lurker","value":"Wayangs gain a +2 racial bonus on Perception and Stealth checks."},{"type":"Shadow Magic","value":"Wayangs add +1 to the save DC of shadow subschool spells they cast. Wayangs with a Charisma score of 11 or higher gain the following spell-like abilities: 1/ day-ghost sound, pass without trace, ventriloquism (caster level equals the wayang's level, saves are Charisma-based)."},{"type":"Shadow Resistance","value":"Wayangs get a +2 racial bonus on saves against spells of the shadow subschool."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Wayangs begin play speaking Common and Wayang. Those with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following: any human language, Abyssal, Aklo, Draconic, Goblin, and Infernal."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"blowgun with 20 darts, dagger"},{"name":"Weasel","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"alignment":"N","size":"Tiny","type":"animal","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 natural, +2 size","hp":4,"hd":"1d8","hdvalue":1,"fort":2,"ref":4,"will":1,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +4 (1d3-4 plus attach)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","strength":3,"dexterity":15,"constitution":10,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":5,"baseatk":0,"cmb":0,"cmd":6,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":10},{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":14}],"environments":["temperate hills"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Presented here are the base animal statistics for all of the most commonly used familiars-of course, these statistics can also be used for normal animals as well. Small animals like these use Dexterity to modify Climb and Swim checks.","group":"Familiar","source":"PFRPG Bestiary","special_abilities":[{"type":"Attach","kind":"Ex","details":"When a weasel hits with a bite attack, it automatically grapples its foe, inflicting automatic bite damage each round."}],"description":"Weasels are predators content with raiding chicken coops or attacking pets when they encounter civilization.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Wyrwood","cr":0.5,"xp":200,"race":"Wyrwood","classes":[{"class":"rogue","level":1}],"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"construct","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 armor, +3 Dex, +1 size","hp":18,"hd":"1d8+10","hdvalue":1,"fort":0,"ref":5,"will":1,"save_mods":"Ref: +1 vs. traps","immunities":["construct traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"dagger +3 (1d3+2/19-20)","ranged":"dagger +4 (1d3+2/19-20)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"sneak attack","details":"+1d6"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":17,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":0,"cmb":1,"cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Deft Hands"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":7},{"skill":"Appraise","value":6},{"skill":"Climb","value":6},{"skill":"Craft (alchemy)","value":6},{"skill":"Disable Device","value":9},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":7},{"skill":"Knowledge (dungeoneering)","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":5},{"skill":"Sleight of Hand","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Use Magic Device","value":2}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"trapfinding","details":"+1"}],"environments":["any ruins","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","cabal (2-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Though obviously a wooden construct, this small, nimble creature moves fluidly and purposefully.","source":"PFRPG Bestiary 4","description":"This relatively young race was created by a cabal of wizards who needed nimble and skilled construct servants to do their dirty work. This cabal was interested in creating magical machines, so they built their tools to be small, agile, and clever. Over time, the cabal pushed the limits too far and created minions with free will, and these first wyrwoods began to question why they were involved in their masters' petty intrigues. When the wizards forced the wyrwoods to fight each other as proxies for the wizards themselves, the constructs instead killed their creators and stole the notes on how to create more of their kind, allowing them to perpetuate their race. Wyrwoods are secretive and somewhat xenophobic, and can seem cold and calculating to others-a trait that could earn respect or enmity, depending on the audience. Purely rational and unemotional, wyrwoods see the survival of their race as their primary goal. Even the rare wyrwood who understands the point of empathy and altruism sees such concepts as mere tools to help ensure its own survival or that of its people. All wyrwoods know how to create more of their kind, and they keep the details of their construction process secret from other creatures to make sure their race is never again enslaved. Most wyrwoods are wizards or rogues. The few who are inclined to worship tend to revere lawful deities with strict codes of behavior, which they follow to the letter rather than the spirit.","as_character":[{"description":"Wyrwood are defined by their class levels-they don't possess racial Hit Dice. All wyrwoods have the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Dexterity, +2 Intelligence, -2 Charisma: Wyrwoods are quick and calculating. As constructs, wyrwoods don't have a Constitution score. Normal Speed: Wyrwoods have a base speed of 30 ft."},{"type":"Small","value":"Wyrwoods are Small creatures and gain a +1 size bonus on attack rolls, a -1 penalty on combat maneuver checks and to combat maneuver defense, a +2 bonus on Fly checks, and a +4 size bonus on Stealth checks."},{"type":"Darkvision 60 ft.","value":"Wyrwoods can see in the dark up to 60 ft."},{"type":"Low-Light Vision","value":"Wyrwoods have low-light vision."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Wyrwoods speak Common. A wyrwood with a high Intelligence score can choose from the following: Draconic, Dwarven, Elven, Gnome, Goblin, or Orc."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"daggers [2], leather armor, thieves' tools"}];
